      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-242863",
      ":question/problem": "Consider a circle $C_{1}$ of radius $\\frac{1}{4}$ centered at $A(-5,-3)$ and a circle $C_{2}$ of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at $B(2,-7)$. Prove that $C_{1}$ and $C_{2}$ are similar.\n\nLet $U$ be the unit circle centered at the origin.\n\nFirst, we will show that\n$C_{1}\u223cU$.\nFor the circle $C_{1}$ of radius $\\frac{1}{4}$ centered at $A(-5,-3)$, applying a translation of\n{{field-1}}\nand\n{{field-2}}\nfollowed by a dilation of {{field-3}}\nmaps $C_{1}$ onto $U$."
